WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.330
There are actually two net neutralities.
There's the original idea, and then

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:12.780
there's what it's morphed into. The
original idea, which a lot of people

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.820
agreed with, is that customers should be
allowed to do what they want to when

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.480
they're using Internet services: Connect
whatever equipment they want to; get to

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.310
whatever services are out there.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:26.970
And, you stop to think about it, it makes a
lot of sense. Would you really want

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:31.769
someone saying you can't use Netflix on
your broadband connection? Or you can't

00:00:31.769 --> 00:00:35.670
use that iPhone because I'm fighting
with Apple over something? But what it

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:39.989
has morphed into is government
restrictions on what customers can get.

00:00:39.989 --> 00:00:43.590
And it's not done by saying, "Well,
customers are not allowed to buy this or

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:48.180
buy that". Instead it's saying broadband
providers cannot provide those services

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.649
to customers.

00:00:49.649 --> 00:00:53.460
One of the motivations for net
neutrality was to keep Internet service

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.600
providers from throttling traffic. But we
have consumer protection. The Federal

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.550
Trade Commission has the responsibility
for addressing issues where consumers

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:03.239
are told

00:01:03.239 --> 00:01:06.239
"I'm giving you this," but in reality I'm
doing something else.

